%I A400644 #28 Oct 11 2026 23:30:12
%S A400644 0,0,1,1,1,2,2,3,4,5,7,9,12,17,23,31,42,57,77,105,142,193,262,355,482,
%T A400644 653,886,1202,1630,2211,2998,4066,5514,7478,10142,13754,18654,25298,
%U A400644 34309,46530,63104,85582,116067,157410,213480,289522,392651,532514,722197
%N A400644 a(n+1) = round(sqrt(a(n)^2 + a(n-1)^2 + a(n-2)^2)).
%C A400644 Consider the three consecutive terms a(n-2), a(n-1), a(n) as Cartesian coordinates x, y, z. Before rounding, sqrt(x^2+y^2+z^2) is their Euclidean distance from the origin; equivalently, it is the space diagonal of a rectangular cuboid with side lengths x, y, z. A063827 is the analog two-term nearest-integer Euclidean-norm recurrence.
%C A400644 Numerical evidence suggests that the ratio of consecutive terms (a(n+1)/a(n)) tends to 1.356203065626295... which is the largest positive real root of x^6-x^4-x^2-1=0. Setting x^2=t gives t^3-t^2-t-1 which yields the tribonacci constant (A058265) as the largest positive root.
%t A400644 RecurrenceTable[{a[n+1] == Round[Sqrt[a[n]^2 + a[n-1]^2 + a[n-2]^2]], a[1] == a[2] == 0, a[3] == 1}, a, {n, 1, 49}] (* _Amiram Eldar_, Oct 05 2026 *)
%o A400644 (Python)
%o A400644 from math import isqrt
%o A400644 def A400644_list(N):
%o A400644     a = [0, 0, 1][:N]
%o A400644     while len(a) < N:
%o A400644         s = sum(x*x for x in a[-3:])
%o A400644         r = isqrt(s)
%o A400644         a.append(r + (s > r*r + r))
%o A400644     return a
%Y A400644 Cf. A000073 (the tribonacci numbers), A063827 (the analog two-term nearest-integer Euclidean-norm recurrence), A058265 (the tribonacci constant, whose square root is the conjectured limiting ratio of consecutive terms), A400645 (the analog four-term recurrence based on the indefinite quadratic form).
%K A400644 nonn,easy,new
%O A400644 1,6
%A A400644 _Bence Bernáth_, Oct 02 2026
